    {
      "ts": "2026-03-03T16:40:17.751102-08:00",
      "seq": 4,
      "player": "MstLg Stats",
      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "Current hand (7 cards):\n- **Double Vision** {3}{R}{R}: Copy your first instant or sorcery each turn. Strong value engine, but expensive.\n- **Hungry Flames** {2}{R}: Deals 3 damage to a creature and 2 to a player/planeswalker. Flexible removal + burn.\n- **Thrill of Possibility** {1}{R}: Discard a card to draw two. Card selection and filtering.\n- **Thriving Bluff** (Land): Enters tapped, adds {R} or another color.\n- **Thriving Moor** (Land): Enters tapped, adds {B} or another color.\n- **Tinybones, Trinket Thief** {1}{B}: Draws when opponents discard, but expensive finisher.\n- **Wight of Precinct Six** {1}{B}: Graveyard synergy, but weak stats.\n\n**Mulligan Analysis:**\n- **Mana Curve:** Only 1 land in hand (2 if you count both Thriving lands, but they enter tapped). This is a **critical issue**—you risk being mana-screwed if you keep.\n- **Playable Turn 1:** Thrill of Possibility (if you discard a land) or a tapped Thriving land. Neither is ideal.\n- **Playable Turn 2:** Hungry Flames or Tinybones/Wight if you hit another land. Still shaky.\n- **Playable Turn 3+:** Double Vision is strong, but you need 5 mana to cast it. With only 1-2 lands in hand, the odds of hitting 5 mana by turn 5 are low.\n- **Redundancy:** No early-game plays beyond Thrill of Possibility or a tapped land. If you miss a land drop, you’re stuck.\n\n**Probability of Improving:**\n- A 6-card hand has a ~70% chance of containing 2 lands (assuming 24 lands in a 60-card deck).\n- Keeping this hand gives you only a ~35% chance of hitting 2 lands by turn 2 (hypergeometric distribution).\n- Mulliganing gives you a better shot at a functional curve.\n\n**Verdict:** **Mulligan.** This hand is too risky for a keep. The lack of early-game consistency and reliance on hitting lands is too high a variance.",
      "toolCalls": [
